  "segments": [
    {
      "id": 0,
      "start": 0.36,
      "end": 8.92,
      "text": "Lighthouses were once staffed by keepers who trimmed wicks, wound clockwork, and logged every passing ship through long winter nights on remote rocky coasts.",
      "speaker": 0
    },
    {
      "id": 1,
      "start": 9.72,
      "end": 19,
      "text": "Bread rises because yeast consumes sugars and releases carbon dioxide, which becomes trapped in a stretchy network of gluten formed while the dough is kneaded.",
      "speaker": 1
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "start": 19.56,
      "end": 27.96,
      "text": "The first transatlantic telegraph cable failed after only three weeks, yet it proved that messages could cross an ocean in minutes rather than in many days.",
      "speaker": 0
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "start": 28.84,
      "end": 38.04,
      "text": "Honeybees communicate the direction and distance of flowers through a waggle dance, angling their bodies relative to the sun while vibrating rapidly on the comb.",
      "speaker": 1
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "start": 38.64,
      "end": 47.84,
      "text": "Glaciers carve valleys into a distinctive U-shape, dragging boulders for hundreds of kilometers and leaving behind ridges of gravel called moraines when they retreat.",
      "speaker": 0
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "start": 48.6,
      "end": 58.6,
      "text": "In medieval monasteries, scribes copied manuscripts by candlelight, mixing inks from oak galls and iron salts, and occasionally scribbling complaints in the margins.",
      "speaker": 1
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "start": 59.24,
      "end": 67.319,
      "text": "Octopuses can change both the color and the texture of their skin within a fraction of a second, even though most species are thought to be color blind.",
      "speaker": 0
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "start": 68.16,
      "end": 77.28,
      "text": "Early railway timetables forced towns to adopt standard time, replacing dozens of local clocks that had each been set by the position of the noon sun.",
      "speaker": 1
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "start": 77.88,
      "end": 88.08,
      "text": "Volcanic soils are often remarkably fertile, which explains why farmers return to the slopes of active mountains despite the obvious and recurring danger. Paper",
      "speaker": 0
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "start": 88.2,
      "end": 96.039,
      "text": "money first appeared in China more than a thousand years ago, when merchants grew tired of carrying heavy strings of copper and iron coins.",
      "speaker": 1
    }
  ],
